      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2013 Blackwell Publishing Ltd.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "This post hoc analysis from the Coronary Drug Project (CDP) evaluated the effects of niacin vs. placebo on the incidence of new onset type 2 diabetes mellitus (T2DM) and cardiovascular event rates in patients with normal and impaired fasting glucose (IFG)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "The CDP was a randomised, placebo-controlled clinical trial of lipid-modifying agents in men with previous myocardial infarction. Normoglycaemia and IFG were defined as fasting plasma glucose (FPG) < 5.6 mmol/l and FPG ≥ 5.6 but < 7.0 mmol/l, respectively. New onset T2DM was defined by ≥ 1 of the following: clinical diagnosis of T2DM, use of an antihyperglycaemic therapy, or two FPG values ≥ 7.0 mmol/l."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "The incidence of new onset T2DM was higher in patients with IFG (16.5%) compared with those with normoglycaemia (5.4%), and was slightly higher with niacin vs. placebo in both normoglycaemic (6.8% vs. 4.9%; p = 0.07) and IFG (19.8% vs. 15.2%; p = 0.05) patients. Consistent with previous analyses, the cardiovascular benefit of niacin was independent of baseline glycaemic status (normal, IFG, T2DM) and change in fasting glucose level from baseline to year 1."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "Despite a modest increase in risk of new onset T2DM with long-term niacin therapy, there is a potential cardiovascular benefit of niacin."
          }
        ]
      },
